WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.778
This briefing comes from Book 02 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.778 --> 00:00:09.112
Identity and Directory: the move
from Active Directory to Entra ID.

3
00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:10.888
I'll take it through four questions.

4
00:00:10.888 --> 00:00:12.587
Why identity matters now.

5
00:00:12.587 --> 00:00:15.738
What actually changes when a
directory moves to the cloud.

6
00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:17.676
Who has to own which decisions.

7
00:00:17.676 --> 00:00:20.413
And how to do it without
losing the governance that

8
00:00:20.413 --> 00:00:23.412
twenty-five years of Active
Directory quietly carried.

9
00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:26.351
The short version: this is
not a directory upgrade.

10
00:00:26.351 --> 00:00:30.320
It decides whether every
security control after it works.

11
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:31.720
Why.

12
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.432
Federal Zero Trust policy
moved the access decision.

13
00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:41.046
OMB memorandum M-22-09 requires agency
staff to use enterprise-managed

14
00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:45.457
identities and phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication.

15
00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:50.861
NIST Special Publication 800-207
removes implicit trust based

16
00:00:50.861 --> 00:00:54.620
on network location: every
request is evaluated on its own.

17
00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.070
And CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model puts
identity first among its five pillars.

18
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.281
What that means in practice is that the

19
00:01:02.281 --> 00:01:05.182
identity provider enforces
whatever it believes.

20
00:01:05.182 --> 00:01:07.858
Who is this person,
and is that still true?

21
00:01:07.858 --> 00:01:10.645
Which groups and roles
do they hold, and why?

22
00:01:10.645 --> 00:01:13.083
Is the device they're
using managed and healthy?

23
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:17.253
Conditional Access, Privileged
Identity Management and device

24
00:01:17.253 --> 00:01:21.270
compliance all read those facts
and act on them, on every sign-in.

25
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.395
So if the facts are wrong, every policy
built on them is wrong, at machine speed.

26
00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:31.095
That's why moving from Active Directory
to Entra ID isn't a directory upgrade.

27
00:01:31.095 --> 00:01:35.640
It decides whether every later
control has true facts to act on.

28
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.190
Microsoft isn't retiring Active Directory.

29
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.490
It still supports and
develops Windows Server AD.

30
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.327
But every road now points to the
cloud, and the dates are real.

31
00:01:46.327 --> 00:01:51.731
In June 2024 Microsoft
deprecated all versions of NTLM,

32
00:01:51.731 --> 00:01:58.191
and NTLMv1 is removed from Windows
11 24H2 and Windows Server 2025.

33
00:01:58.191 --> 00:02:01.922
On July 1, 2026, Entra ID
began blocking sync from

34
00:02:01.922 --> 00:02:06.515
taking over privileged or
already-mapped cloud accounts.

35
00:02:06.515 --> 00:02:10.516
This month's security update
restricts NTLMv1-derived single

36
00:02:10.516 --> 00:02:14.902
sign-on credentials by default on
devices without Credential Guard.

37
00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:20.855
And by April 7, 2027, every Connect
Sync server must run version 2.6.84.0

38
00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:27.552
or later with application-based
authentication, or synchronization stops.

39
00:02:27.552 --> 00:02:32.132
Behind those dates is a direction:
Cloud Sync replacing Connect Sync,

40
00:02:32.132 --> 00:02:34.703
cloud authentication instead of AD FS,

41
00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:39.202
and sources of authority converted
to the cloud one object at a time.

42
00:02:39.202 --> 00:02:41.264
The trap is the lift and shift.

43
00:02:41.264 --> 00:02:46.605
Synchronize the forest as it stands, and
twenty-five years of organizational units,

44
00:02:46.605 --> 00:02:50.552
stale accounts and undocumented
trusts arrive in the tenant.

45
00:02:50.552 --> 00:02:54.209
Meanwhile the governance
that the hierarchy carried,

46
00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:58.353
who could manage what and where
policy applied, is left behind,

47
00:02:58.353 --> 00:03:02.940
because Entra ID has no organizational
unit (OU) tree to carry it.

48
00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.715
What changes.

49
00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:08.277
These are two different models,
not two versions of one product.

50
00:03:08.277 --> 00:03:14.653
Active Directory is a hierarchy: a forest,
domains, and nested organizational units.

51
00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:17.102
Entra ID is one flat tenant.

52
00:03:17.102 --> 00:03:21.016
Structure comes from attributes
and administrative units instead.

53
00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:25.015
In AD, much of what you are is
where you sit in the OU tree.

54
00:03:25.015 --> 00:03:29.032
In Entra ID it should be attributes
from an authoritative source,

55
00:03:29.032 --> 00:03:31.040
which for the workforce means HR.

56
00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.978
Policy in AD is Group
Policy linked to OUs.

57
00:03:34.978 --> 00:03:40.052
In the cloud it's Conditional Access and
Intune, targeted by those attributes.

58
00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:43.325
The protocols change too: Kerberos,

59
00:03:43.325 --> 00:03:49.078
NTLM and LDAP give way to OpenID
Connect, OAuth, SAML and SCIM.

60
00:03:49.078 --> 00:03:52.745
Standing Domain Admin membership
gives way to least-privilege

61
00:03:52.745 --> 00:03:56.828
roles activated just in time through
Privileged Identity Management.

62
00:03:56.828 --> 00:04:01.291
And management moves from the
ActiveDirectory module to Microsoft Graph.

63
00:04:01.291 --> 00:04:03.490
The key idea is the bottom line.

64
00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:07.727
In Active Directory, governance is
implied by where an object sits.

65
00:04:07.727 --> 00:04:10.943
In Entra ID it has to
be stated explicitly,

66
00:04:10.943 --> 00:04:14.620
as data and rules, or it
simply doesn't exist.

67
00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.408
The second thing that changes
is the size of the job.

68
00:04:18.408 --> 00:04:21.432
Most migration plans
count users and groups.

69
00:04:21.432 --> 00:04:24.882
But Active Directory has also
been holding the estate together.

70
00:04:24.882 --> 00:04:31.169
DNS, DHCP and IP address management,
with the zones applications depend on.

71
00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:36.457
Certificate Services, behind smart
cards, Wi-Fi, VPN and servers.

72
00:04:36.457 --> 00:04:40.982
RADIUS through the Network Policy
Server for Wi-Fi and VPN sign-in.

73
00:04:40.982 --> 00:04:44.445
Applications that bind to
the directory over LDAP.

74
00:04:44.445 --> 00:04:47.945
The sync engine itself,
which is a tier zero asset.

75
00:04:47.945 --> 00:04:51.645
Devices that are domain-joined
and governed by Group Policy.

76
00:04:51.645 --> 00:04:55.308
Time, because Kerberos depends
on synchronized clocks.

77
00:04:55.308 --> 00:04:59.157
And file services, whose
permissions are bound to AD groups.

78
00:04:59.157 --> 00:05:02.582
None of those move just because
users sync to the cloud.

79
00:05:02.582 --> 00:05:07.118
Some, like the NPS extension for
Entra multifactor authentication,

80
00:05:07.118 --> 00:05:10.358
are useful interim steps
that still depend on AD.

81
00:05:10.358 --> 00:05:15.396
The program's answer is an adapter for
each dependency: an owner, a target, a

82
00:05:15.396 --> 00:05:17.915
cutover test and a validation check,

83
00:05:17.915 --> 00:05:21.720
retired in priority order
rather than all at once.

84
00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.508
The third change is delegation.

85
00:05:24.508 --> 00:05:28.982
In Active Directory, who can manage
what was largely expressed by OUs.

86
00:05:28.982 --> 00:05:34.320
Entra ID has no OUs, so delegation
has to be rebuilt on purpose.

87
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.532
The program uses three tiers.

88
00:05:36.532 --> 00:05:41.558
Tier zero is tenant-wide control:
Global Administrator and similar roles,

89
00:05:41.558 --> 00:05:45.183
plus break-glass accounts,
all activated just in time.

90
00:05:45.183 --> 00:05:50.145
Tier one is service administration,
such as Exchange, Intune and security.

91
00:05:50.145 --> 00:05:52.832
Tier two is departmental administration,

92
00:05:52.832 --> 00:05:56.057
like the help desk, scoped
to administrative units.

93
00:05:56.057 --> 00:06:00.733
The platform sets real limits, and the
old design documents got several wrong.

94
00:06:00.733 --> 00:06:03.145
Administrative units can't be nested.

95
00:06:03.145 --> 00:06:06.158
Groups that hold roles can't
have dynamic membership.

96
00:06:06.158 --> 00:06:09.519
Reader roles like Global Reader
apply to the whole tenant.

97
00:06:09.519 --> 00:06:14.882
And restricted management is chosen when a
unit is created; it can't be added later.

98
00:06:14.882 --> 00:06:19.128
It also takes those users and
groups out of Entra ID Governance:

99
00:06:19.128 --> 00:06:22.557
no access reviews or
lifecycle workflows for them.

100
00:06:22.557 --> 00:06:27.545
So the program's decision is that administrative
units are unrestricted by default.

101
00:06:27.545 --> 00:06:31.657
Restricted management is a per-unit
choice for sensitive accounts,

102
00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:34.960
recorded in the registry
before the unit exists.

103
00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:40.048
One more thing changes, and users
will feel it: how they sign in.

104
00:06:40.048 --> 00:06:45.662
OMB M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication

105
00:06:45.662 --> 00:06:49.323
for agency staff, and it names
PIV as the simplest route.

106
00:06:49.323 --> 00:06:51.534
That reorders the usual roadmap.

107
00:06:51.534 --> 00:06:55.468
Earlier plans treated smart-card
sign-in as the last phase,

108
00:06:55.468 --> 00:06:57.435
for high-security users only.

109
00:06:57.435 --> 00:07:01.075
But a federal workforce already
carries PIV or CAC cards,

110
00:07:01.075 --> 00:07:06.341
so Entra certificate-based authentication
is the fastest path: the credential is

111
00:07:06.341 --> 00:07:11.685
already issued, and it works directly
against Entra ID without a federation server.

112
00:07:11.685 --> 00:07:13.385
The book runs four waves.

113
00:07:13.385 --> 00:07:18.410
Privileged accounts first, on FIDO2
security keys, or their PIV card.

114
00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:23.697
Then everyone who holds a PIV or CAC card,
through certificate-based authentication.

115
00:07:23.697 --> 00:07:27.786
Then managed Windows devices,
with Windows Hello for Business.

116
00:07:27.786 --> 00:07:31.043
And finally everyone else,
including mobile users,

117
00:07:31.043 --> 00:07:33.835
with passkeys in Microsoft Authenticator.

118
00:07:33.835 --> 00:07:38.173
All four meet Entra's built-in
phishing-resistant authentication strength.

119
00:07:38.173 --> 00:07:41.423
Some things go away: SMS
and voice codes for staff,

120
00:07:41.423 --> 00:07:45.892
which the memo requires agencies
to stop using for routine access,

121
00:07:45.892 --> 00:07:50.036
and legacy protocols that send
a password with every request.

122
00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:52.774
Those are found in the
sign-in logs, fixed,

123
00:07:52.774 --> 00:07:55.597
then blocked, and watched for thirty days.

124
00:07:55.597 --> 00:07:58.398
One thing stays on: password hash sync.

125
00:07:58.398 --> 00:08:01.179
It enables leaked-credential detection,

126
00:08:01.179 --> 00:08:05.790
and it's the manual fallback if
pass-through agents or AD FS fail.

127
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:07.140
Who.

128
00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:11.519
A directory migration fails more
often on ownership than on technology,

129
00:08:11.519 --> 00:08:14.877
so the book separates who
decides from who does the work.

130
00:08:14.877 --> 00:08:20.602
Deciding: the CIO and executive sponsor
set scope, funding and sequence.

131
00:08:20.602 --> 00:08:24.303
The CISO sets the control
requirements the target has to meet.

132
00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:28.997
And the authorizing official accepts
any risk the program proposes to carry

133
00:08:28.997 --> 00:08:33.252
instead of remove, such as a service
account that can't be converted yet.

134
00:08:33.252 --> 00:08:38.347
Building and running: identity
engineering designs and builds the target:

135
00:08:38.347 --> 00:08:43.277
sync, authentication, Conditional
Access, PIM and administrative units.

136
00:08:43.277 --> 00:08:47.991
AD administrators clean the source
and, at the end, retire it.

137
00:08:47.991 --> 00:08:52.515
The ISSO assembles the evidence
and tracks findings to closure.

138
00:08:52.515 --> 00:08:57.204
Supplying and accepting: HR supplies
the facts the directory can't

139
00:08:57.204 --> 00:09:01.978
verify on its own, which is the
subject of the HR-driven identity book.

140
00:09:01.978 --> 00:09:06.089
Application owners accept test
results and cutover windows.

141
00:09:06.089 --> 00:09:11.353
And the help desk and users carry
recovery, registration and wave readiness.

142
00:09:11.353 --> 00:09:13.127
One owner is usually missing.

143
00:09:13.127 --> 00:09:16.916
Microsoft's native tools each
own a slice of the picture.

144
00:09:16.916 --> 00:09:21.190
Nobody owns the gaps between them
unless the program names someone.

145
00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:22.553
How.

146
00:09:22.553 --> 00:09:25.152
The book's How chapters follow five steps.

147
00:09:25.152 --> 00:09:26.365
One, discover.

148
00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:30.828
Start with read-only access,
which every employee already has,

149
00:09:30.828 --> 00:09:35.203
and learn the topology, OU tree,
groups and Group Policy links.

150
00:09:35.203 --> 00:09:39.427
Then ask for narrow, time-bound access
for only what you couldn't see.

151
00:09:39.427 --> 00:09:44.297
Two, assess: the full forest inventory,
including certificate templates,

152
00:09:44.297 --> 00:09:49.653
trusts and delegation, without ever
requesting the rights that expose secrets.

153
00:09:49.653 --> 00:09:51.377
Three, build the target.

154
00:09:51.377 --> 00:09:55.779
The book recommends Cloud Sync unless
a Connect-only requirement applies,

155
00:09:55.779 --> 00:10:00.819
password hash sync everywhere, Privileged
Identity Management for every admin role,

156
00:10:00.819 --> 00:10:03.303
and a tested Conditional Access baseline.

157
00:10:03.303 --> 00:10:08.390
Four, migrate in waves: phishing-resistant
authentication, including PIV and CAC

158
00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:13.628
through certificate-based authentication,
and legacy authentication turned off.

159
00:10:13.628 --> 00:10:17.025
Five, prove it: drift checks
against the baseline and

160
00:10:17.025 --> 00:10:20.739
an evidence bundle the
authorizing official can rely on.

161
00:10:20.739 --> 00:10:22.990
A few rules hold for every wave.

162
00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:26.428
Never enforce an untested
policy on all users.

163
00:10:26.428 --> 00:10:30.940
Keep two cloud-only break-glass accounts
on phishing-resistant credentials.

164
00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:34.802
Convert service accounts to
managed or workload identities.

165
00:10:34.802 --> 00:10:39.330
And treat every closure as unproven
until it carries its evidence.

166
00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.743
Chapter 11 puts the steps together
in a reference deployment.

167
00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:49.232
It's a framing pattern, not a real engagement:
a civilian agency with two forests,

168
00:10:49.232 --> 00:10:53.117
a trust between them, and a
fleet of SQL Server instances.

169
00:10:53.117 --> 00:10:56.230
The figures are illustrative;
the mechanics are real.

170
00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:58.143
It runs in five phases.

171
00:10:58.143 --> 00:11:02.517
Discover: a read-only survey of
both forests, including the service

172
00:11:02.517 --> 00:11:07.606
accounts and every service principal
name the SQL Server work will depend on.

173
00:11:07.606 --> 00:11:12.780
Normalize: an organizational registry built
from that survey and checked against the

174
00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.727
agency's own chart, with
a finding raised for every

175
00:11:15.727 --> 00:11:18.818
account the registry can't
attribute to a real owner.

176
00:11:18.818 --> 00:11:23.589
Map: one adapter per surface, and
four carry most of the risk here:

177
00:11:23.589 --> 00:11:27.718
synchronization, certificates,
RADIUS and SQL Server.

178
00:11:27.718 --> 00:11:31.177
Migrate: every change recorded
with who asked for it,

179
00:11:31.177 --> 00:11:34.556
on what authority, and a
hash of the resulting state.

180
00:11:34.556 --> 00:11:38.149
And Validate: the before and
after inventories compared,

181
00:11:38.149 --> 00:11:41.055
and every drift finding
closed with evidence.

182
00:11:41.055 --> 00:11:43.292
What comes out is an evidence bundle.

183
00:11:43.292 --> 00:11:47.407
It goes to the authorizing official
and the security control assessor,

184
00:11:47.407 --> 00:11:51.731
who authorize the agency's own system
under the Risk Management Framework.

185
00:11:51.731 --> 00:11:53.643
And it belongs to the agency.

186
00:11:53.643 --> 00:11:56.917
When the integrator leaves,
the registry, the schemas,

187
00:11:56.917 --> 00:12:01.950
the findings and the evidence stay
behind for the next authorization cycle.

188
00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:03.925
So, four asks.

189
00:12:03.925 --> 00:12:08.102
Name the owners: the sponsor, the
CISO, the authorizing official,

190
00:12:08.102 --> 00:12:13.225
the identity engineering lead, and someone
who owns the gaps between the tools.

191
00:12:13.225 --> 00:12:15.325
Start read-only discovery now.

192
00:12:15.325 --> 00:12:18.810
It needs no elevated access
to begin, and the request for

193
00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:22.825
more access then follows the
evidence rather than preceding it.

194
00:12:22.825 --> 00:12:24.313
Put the dates on the plan.

195
00:12:24.313 --> 00:12:28.776
Connect Sync has to be
upgraded by April 7, 2027.

196
00:12:28.776 --> 00:12:35.187
NTLM and legacy authentication should come
off wave by wave, not in one weekend.

197
00:12:35.187 --> 00:12:38.420
And agree what done means:
every control backed by

198
00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:41.888
evidence an assessor can read,
not a status in a ticket.

199
00:12:41.888 --> 00:12:46.150
Identity matters because it is now
where every access decision is made.

200
00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:50.186
Getting it right once, while the
platform is being rebuilt anyway,

201
00:12:50.186 --> 00:12:53.325
is far cheaper than rebuilding
the same gaps twice.

202
00:12:53.325 --> 00:12:56.961
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 02 has the full

203
00:12:56.961 --> 00:13:00.975
argument and the runbooks, with
every external claim footnoted.

204
00:13:00.975 --> 00:13:02.876
Book 03, HR-Driven Identity,

205
00:13:02.876 --> 00:13:07.025
picks up where the facts in
the directory should come from.

206
00:13:07.025 --> 00:13:07.860
Thank you.
